module gunbc.machine_intake.supermicro_h11ssl_unit_identity

import extdeps.network.mac { Eui48Address, mac_address }

// THE ONE PLACE EACH H11SSL UNIT'S BMC MAC IS WRITTEN. Our units are keyed by their BMC's MAC, and
// every receipt about a unit names it through these rows. They live apart from the receipts so the
// first-contact receipt, the release observations and the unit observations can all read them without
// an import cycle.
//
// HOW EACH MAC WAS COME BY, because the three were not come by the same way: unit1's and unit2's were
// read OFF THE BMC ITSELF at first contact (the console SOL transcript of the KVM-over-IP session,
// gunbc.machine_intake.supermicro_h11ssl_first_contact). Unit3's was NEVER READ OFF THE BMC: it is the
// link address the BMC answered with on the fleet LAN, seen in srv1's neighbour table on 2026-11-15
// (smart-crane-730, read-only, reported in msg_01a71ff1; the same read answered REACHABLE for the
// address, 192.168.1.249). A neighbour-table MAC is what the wire carried, not what the board claims
// for itself, so it identifies the unit without being a read of the unit. The BMC's own read of its
// MAC -- `ipmitool lan print`, or Redfish EthernetInterfaces -- is the read that would retire that gap.
data supermicro_h11ssl_unit1_bmc_mac: Eui48Address = mac_address(octet1: 172, octet2: 31, octet3: 107, octet4: 227, octet5: 245, octet6: 123)
data supermicro_h11ssl_unit2_bmc_mac: Eui48Address = mac_address(octet1: 172, octet2: 31, octet3: 107, octet4: 227, octet5: 246, octet6: 34)
data supermicro_h11ssl_unit3_bmc_mac: Eui48Address = mac_address(octet1: 172, octet2: 31, octet3: 107, octet4: 230, octet5: 114, octet6: 22)

// UNIT 4 (john, host-ba61f9, 2026-11-26): ac:1f:6b:e6:76:a2, the BMC at 192.168.1.251, as reported by the session
// that installed the unit by hand (gunbc.machine_intake.supermicro_h11ssl_unit4_hand_install_receipt). The step log
// committed beside that receipt does not record which read produced it, so -- like unit3's -- it identifies the
// unit without being a committed read of the BMC; `ipmitool lan print 1` in band is the read that would pin it.
data supermicro_h11ssl_unit4_bmc_mac: Eui48Address = mac_address(octet1: 172, octet2: 31, octet3: 107, octet4: 230, octet5: 118, octet6: 162)
